WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:01.016
Lesson two point one.

2
00:00:01.295 --> 00:00:03.388
Where the songs come from, and which model

3
00:00:03.388 --> 00:00:04.271
learned from which.

4
00:00:08.173 --> 00:00:10.325
Every song in this topic is a MIDI file.

5
00:00:10.647 --> 00:00:12.476
A MIDI file holds no sound at all.

6
00:00:12.799 --> 00:00:15.380
It's a list of timed instructions: at this

7
00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:17.693
moment, on this instrument, start this

8
00:00:17.693 --> 00:00:18.661
note, this hard.

9
00:00:19.311 --> 00:00:21.572
A synthesiser turns the instructions into

10
00:00:21.572 --> 00:00:23.887
sound, so the same file sounds different

11
00:00:23.887 --> 00:00:24.695
on every synth.

12
00:00:25.018 --> 00:00:27.602
The models never hear any of it: they read

13
00:00:27.602 --> 00:00:28.518
the instructions.

14
00:00:28.942 --> 00:00:31.376
A big collection of files like this, used

15
00:00:31.376 --> 00:00:33.202
to train and test, is a corpus.

16
00:00:33.534 --> 00:00:35.857
This project trains on two corpora, and

17
00:00:35.857 --> 00:00:36.964
judges with a third.

18
00:00:37.296 --> 00:00:38.900
Here's why each one is there.

19
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:45.329
The main corpus is the Lakh MIDI Dataset,

20
00:00:45.329 --> 00:00:47.872
built by Colin Raffel for his PhD thesis

21
00:00:47.872 --> 00:00:48.988
in twenty sixteen.

22
00:00:49.532 --> 00:00:51.833
He crawled the web for every MIDI file he

23
00:00:51.833 --> 00:00:52.436
could find.

24
00:00:52.765 --> 00:00:55.121
Nobody curated them: they're whatever

25
00:00:55.121 --> 00:00:57.313
hobbyists and karaoke sites uploaded,

26
00:00:57.313 --> 00:00:59.340
roughly from nineteen ninety-five to

27
00:00:59.340 --> 00:00:59.943
twenty ten.

28
00:01:00.415 --> 00:01:02.018
Our archive holds a hundred and

29
00:01:02.018 --> 00:01:04.173
seventy-eight thousand, five hundred and

30
00:01:04.173 --> 00:01:04.975
sixty-one files.

31
00:01:05.276 --> 00:01:07.581
After a filter, fifty-two thousand, seven

32
00:01:07.581 --> 00:01:09.585
hundred and ninety-three songs are left

33
00:01:09.585 --> 00:01:11.590
that have both a guitar part and a drum

34
00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:12.943
part, about three in ten.

35
00:01:13.243 --> 00:01:15.649
That's version three of the corpus; lesson

36
00:01:15.649 --> 00:01:17.603
two point two is the filter, and the

37
00:01:17.603 --> 00:01:18.054
versions.

38
00:01:18.471 --> 00:01:18.917
Why Lakh?

39
00:01:19.214 --> 00:01:21.245
Because nothing else has guitar and drum

40
00:01:21.245 --> 00:01:23.375
parts written for the same song, at this

41
00:01:23.375 --> 00:01:23.672
scale.

42
00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:26.199
And with about fifty thousand songs, there

43
00:01:26.199 --> 00:01:28.180
was room to throw out anything doubtful

44
00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:29.864
and keep only what we could trust.

45
00:01:33.317 --> 00:01:35.489
The genre drummers are drummers for one

46
00:01:35.489 --> 00:01:37.770
genre each: a Rock drummer, a Jazz

47
00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:39.345
drummer, a Country drummer.

48
00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:42.005
Each one has to be checked on songs of its

49
00:01:42.005 --> 00:01:44.232
genre that it never trained on: the

50
00:01:44.232 --> 00:01:45.101
validation side.

51
00:01:45.623 --> 00:01:47.278
Lakh's genre labels are thin.

52
00:01:47.621 --> 00:01:50.075
On its validation side: a hundred and

53
00:01:50.075 --> 00:01:52.529
fifty-two Rock songs, eighty-four Pop,

54
00:01:52.529 --> 00:01:54.641
twenty-five Country, fourteen Jazz.

55
00:01:54.984 --> 00:01:57.266
Twenty-five songs is too few to judge a

56
00:01:57.266 --> 00:01:58.351
Country drummer by.

57
00:01:58.843 --> 00:02:01.074
So on the fifteenth of October a second

58
00:02:01.074 --> 00:02:03.864
corpus came in, with labels on every file:

59
00:02:03.864 --> 00:02:04.199
XMIDI.

60
00:02:04.534 --> 00:02:06.933
Its validation side holds four hundred and

61
00:02:06.933 --> 00:02:09.388
seventy-seven Rock songs, six hundred and

62
00:02:09.388 --> 00:02:11.731
twenty-three Country, and three hundred

63
00:02:11.731 --> 00:02:12.568
and eight Jazz.

64
00:02:16.173 --> 00:02:18.374
XMIDI comes from a twenty twenty-five

65
00:02:18.374 --> 00:02:20.516
paper called XMusic, by authors at

66
00:02:20.516 --> 00:02:20.979
Tencent.

67
00:02:21.327 --> 00:02:23.527
It's a hundred and eight thousand and

68
00:02:23.527 --> 00:02:26.133
twenty-three MIDI files, also crawled from

69
00:02:26.133 --> 00:02:26.596
the web.

70
00:02:27.302 --> 00:02:29.890
Every file carries its labels in its name:

71
00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:32.101
one of eleven emotions, and one of six

72
00:02:32.101 --> 00:02:32.478
genres.

73
00:02:32.963 --> 00:02:35.343
And the labels are human: ten trained

74
00:02:35.343 --> 00:02:37.835
annotators, every file checked by at least

75
00:02:37.835 --> 00:02:38.610
three of them.

76
00:02:38.942 --> 00:02:40.437
Not a guess from a program.

77
00:02:40.769 --> 00:02:42.818
Lesson two point five compares label

78
00:02:42.818 --> 00:02:43.260
sources.

79
00:02:43.889 --> 00:02:44.875
It has its own catches.

80
00:02:45.132 --> 00:02:47.017
After our filter, and dropping songs that

81
00:02:47.017 --> 00:02:48.603
twin our Lakh training side, forty

82
00:02:48.603 --> 00:02:50.316
thousand, six hundred and ninety-four

83
00:02:50.316 --> 00:02:50.959
songs are left.

84
00:02:51.216 --> 00:02:53.144
Nine in ten songs were saved at one tempo,

85
00:02:53.144 --> 00:02:54.987
a hundred and nineteen beats per minute,

86
00:02:54.987 --> 00:02:56.101
so the true tempo is gone.

87
00:02:56.358 --> 00:02:58.200
And there are no song identities, so the

88
00:02:58.200 --> 00:02:59.957
split groups songs whose guitar melodies

89
00:02:59.957 --> 00:03:01.928
match, twins, and keeps each group on one

90
00:03:01.928 --> 00:03:02.142
side.

91
00:03:05.577 --> 00:03:07.914
The third dataset is Groove MIDI, from

92
00:03:07.914 --> 00:03:09.852
Google Magenta in twenty nineteen.

93
00:03:10.194 --> 00:03:12.588
Ten real drummers on an electronic kit,

94
00:03:12.588 --> 00:03:15.096
playing to a click: eleven hundred and

95
00:03:15.096 --> 00:03:17.433
fifty files, thirteen point five eight

96
00:03:17.433 --> 00:03:17.775
hours.

97
00:03:18.239 --> 00:03:20.282
It has no guitar, so it can't teach a

98
00:03:20.282 --> 00:03:21.967
drummer that listens to a guitar.

99
00:03:22.274 --> 00:03:23.398
It's never trained on.

100
00:03:23.704 --> 00:03:25.901
Instead it's the judge: the battery's

101
00:03:25.901 --> 00:03:27.995
picture of how real drummers play, two

102
00:03:27.995 --> 00:03:29.987
thousand, four hundred and forty-one

103
00:03:29.987 --> 00:03:32.132
windows from a hundred and sixty-eight of

104
00:03:32.132 --> 00:03:34.022
its two hundred and three rock beats.

105
00:03:34.442 --> 00:03:36.620
Lakh's parts were typed into sequencers by

106
00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:38.747
hobbyists, so their timing and loudness

107
00:03:38.747 --> 00:03:40.570
are mostly the sequencer's, not a

108
00:03:40.570 --> 00:03:41.076
drummer's.

109
00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:43.457
Groove is the only judge of how hard and

110
00:03:43.457 --> 00:03:44.723
how loosely a human hits.

111
00:03:45.027 --> 00:03:47.204
A feel model trained on Groove was planned

112
00:03:47.204 --> 00:03:49.331
and never built, so the live drums play

113
00:03:49.331 --> 00:03:51.610
every hit at velocity one hundred, exactly

114
00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:52.218
on the grid.

115
00:03:55.717 --> 00:03:58.163
Now, which model learned from which.

116
00:03:58.549 --> 00:04:01.189
Four drummers trained on Lakh, version

117
00:04:01.189 --> 00:04:04.022
three: two S4D models, S128 on about

118
00:04:04.022 --> 00:04:06.597
fourteen thousand songs and S128full on

119
00:04:06.597 --> 00:04:09.366
all forty-seven and a half thousand, the

120
00:04:09.366 --> 00:04:11.490
transformer T128, and the LSTM.

121
00:04:12.077 --> 00:04:14.719
Three genre drummers trained on XMIDI,

122
00:04:14.719 --> 00:04:17.554
each a transformer at T128's settings, on

123
00:04:17.554 --> 00:04:20.711
one genre's songs: Rock, Jazz and Country.

124
00:04:21.098 --> 00:04:23.676
The bass models trained on XMIDI's rock

125
00:04:23.676 --> 00:04:24.320
songs too.

126
00:04:24.889 --> 00:04:27.714
There's one bridge between them: the Lakh

127
00:04:27.714 --> 00:04:30.298
T128 is scored on XMIDI's validation songs

128
00:04:30.298 --> 00:04:32.642
for each genre, without any training

129
00:04:32.642 --> 00:04:33.002
there.

130
00:04:33.363 --> 00:04:35.766
It's the all-genre reference each genre

131
00:04:35.766 --> 00:04:37.269
drummer is compared with.

132
00:04:40.807 --> 00:04:42.479
Here's the whole lesson on one card.

133
00:04:42.757 --> 00:04:45.080
Songs: fifty-two thousand from Lakh, forty

134
00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:47.077
thousand from XMIDI, and Groove's eleven

135
00:04:47.077 --> 00:04:49.074
hundred and fifty files, two hundred and

136
00:04:49.074 --> 00:04:50.839
three of them rock beats in four four.

137
00:04:51.118 --> 00:04:53.580
Genres: under a fifth of Lakh is labelled;

138
00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:54.508
every XMIDI file is.

139
00:04:54.787 --> 00:04:57.017
The labels: Lakh's come through a match to

140
00:04:57.017 --> 00:04:58.828
real recordings, XMIDI's from people.

141
00:04:59.239 --> 00:05:01.959
Tempo: Lakh keeps the real tempos; XMIDI

142
00:05:01.959 --> 00:05:04.039
sits at a hundred and nineteen in nine

143
00:05:04.039 --> 00:05:04.732
songs of ten.

144
00:05:05.052 --> 00:05:07.719
The split: Lakh by song identity, XMIDI by

145
00:05:07.719 --> 00:05:10.119
twin clusters, both ninety, five, five.

146
00:05:10.439 --> 00:05:13.052
And the models: four on Lakh, three genre

147
00:05:13.052 --> 00:05:15.559
drummers on XMIDI, and none on Groove, the

148
00:05:15.559 --> 00:05:15.879
judge.

149
00:05:18.815 --> 00:05:21.227
So: Lakh supplies the guitar and drum

150
00:05:21.227 --> 00:05:23.358
patterns, XMIDI supplies songs with

151
00:05:23.358 --> 00:05:25.601
trustworthy genres, and Groove is the

152
00:05:25.601 --> 00:05:27.676
judge that no model ever learns from.

153
00:05:28.012 --> 00:05:30.592
Next, lesson two point two: how Lakh's

154
00:05:30.592 --> 00:05:32.499
files were filtered down to songs.

